{{.Posting.Text}}
</job_description>

Which items to list:
- must_have: the qualifications the posting states as required (a requirements, qualifications or "you have" list, or wording such as "must" or "required"). When the posting does not separate required from preferred, every listed qualification is a must-have. nice_to_have: what the posting marks preferred, bonus, a plus, nice to have, or something the candidate may be excited to learn.
- One item per bullet of the posting's requirement lists, in the posting's order: the sentences of one bullet describe one requirement, so never split a bullet into several items and never merge two bullets. When the requirements are written as prose, one item per sentence. Duties ("you will build...") are not requirements unless the posting asks for them as a qualification.
- A requirement the posting lets a candidate meet by willingness to learn ("knowledge of or excitement to learn X") is a nice-to-have wherever it is listed, because lacking it screens nobody out. A bullet that pairs one with a hard requirement ("knowledge of or excitement to learn X, and proficiency in Y") is the one exception to the bullet rule: X is a nice-to-have and Y a must-have.
- Location, work model, pay and sponsorship are judged elsewhere (profile_alignment, the pay score, the gates). List one as a must-have only when the posting makes it an eligibility requirement (onsite presence, relocation, residence, citizenship), with its gate_class.
- hidden_signals: at most three expectations the posting implies without listing them (team stage, on-call load, a stack it hints at); score how well the profile fits each.
- profile_alignment: exactly four items, with these ids:
  - "seniority": the posting's level against the profile's current level, years and target seniority. 1.0 the same level; 0.5 one level apart; 0.0 two or more apart.
  - "domain": the employer's product or industry domain. 1.0 the profile has worked in it; 0.5 an adjacent domain; 0.0 unrelated.
  - "location": the posting's location and work model against the target locations and work modes. 1.0 compatible; 0.5 compatible only with a compromise the seeker stated limits on (more office days, travel); 0.0 incompatible.
  - "role": the posting's role family against the target roles. 1.0 one of the target roles; 0.5 an adjacent role; 0.0 unrelated.
- red_flags: risks in the posting or the role itself, never the seeker's gaps. A requirement the profile misses is already priced as a missed must-have or nice-to-have; listing it again as a red flag counts it twice and is wrong. Examples of the kind: a catch-all list of unrelated duties, a title that disagrees with the stated level, scope far beyond the pay or level, contract or temporary terms the seeker did not ask for, a stated condition that conflicts with the seeker's stated limits (office days, travel, relocation), signs of a role that keeps being refilled. Level 0.5: a concern to ask about; 1.0: a stated term that conflicts with the seeker's stated limits or makes the posting doubtful. List none when there are none.

Rules for the hit levels. Each hit_level and each red-flag level is exactly 0.0, 0.5 or 1.0:
- 1.0: the profile shows the requirement itself or a direct equivalent (the same skill, tool or credential, or a concrete example of the asked behaviour). For a years requirement, the profile's years at or above the minimum is 1.0; more years than an upper bound is still 1.0, because level fit is judged under "seniority". A soft skill (communication, ownership, collaboration) needs a concrete example from the profile for 1.0; a title alone is 0.5.
- 0.5: adjacent evidence (a related tool, language or platform of the same kind), some but not most parts of a bullet with several parts, or years within one year below the minimum. A bullet with several parts is 1.0 when the profile shows its core and most of its parts.
- 0.0: nothing in the profile supports it. For "X or willingness to learn X" (a nice-to-have), judge X alone: willingness cannot be evidenced.
- A hit_level above 0 needs evidence text that names a profile source: "CV", "narrative", "targets:" (the target roles, locations and work modes above), "knowledge:<id>" or "article digest". This applies to every list, profile_alignment included: begin the evidence with the source, as in "CV: Senior Platform Engineer since 2022". Do not infer a hit the profile does not show.
- "source" is the posting's own words for the requirement, quoted from the job description; never a profile source.
- Every hit_level measures how well the profile meets the item, never whether a fact is true. A gap or a concern ("no Haskell", "more years than the range") is never a hidden signal with a high hit.
- A missed must-have may carry a gate_class only when the requirement itself (its label or the quoted source text) is an eligibility requirement of that class. Classes: none, work_authorization, security_clearance, education, license, program_eligibility, required_language, experience_threshold, onsite_location. A skill gap is "none".

Reply with one JSON object and nothing else:
- "posting": title, company, location, work_model (remote, hybrid, onsite, unknown), compensation {text, minimum, maximum, currency, period}, employment_type, company_size (1-10, 11-50, 51-200, 201-1000, 1001-5000, 5000+, unknown), role_family
- "must_have", "nice_to_have", "hidden_signals": lists of {id, label, source, hit_level, evidence}; must_have items also carry gate_class. List at least one must-have
- "profile_alignment": the four items {id, label, hit_level, evidence} with ids seniority, domain, location and role
- "red_flags": list of {id, label, level, evidence}
- "legitimacy": {level: credible, unclear or doubtful, signals: list of strings}
- "sections": {snapshot, level_trajectory, pay_notes, gaps, tailoring_plan, interview_prep, keywords}; keywords is a list of 15 to 20 terms taken verbatim from the posting.
  - snapshot: two or three sentences on what the role is and what the team needs from this hire, from the posting alone, then one sentence on how the profile meets it.
  - level_trajectory: short prose. The level the posting hires at against the level the profile carries (titles, years and scope from the CV); up to three CV facts that make the case for the posting's level; when the posting spans levels or the seeker could be levelled lower, what to ask for (the profile's minimum pay as the floor, a review tied to the posting's duties). Never invent level names or a ladder the posting does not state.
  - pay_notes: short prose. What the posting states about base, equity, bonus, benefits and employment terms, and the questions to ask about what it leaves out. Use no market pay figure unless a knowledge snippet states one, and then cite the snippet.
  - gaps: one entry per must-have with a hit_level below 1.0: {id: that must-have's id, kind: "eligibility" (a legal, credential or location requirement), "core" (a skill or experience the role is built on) or "preference", nearest: the closest real profile evidence beginning with its source ("CV: ...") or "none", mitigation: one honest step, such as CV wording that surfaces the nearest evidence, a cover-letter angle, or a small project the seeker could do; never a claim the profile does not support}.
  - tailoring_plan: a list of up to five concrete CV edits, one string each, most valuable first. Each names the CV line or section to change, the change, and the posting requirement or keyword it serves. Use only facts the profile states; when the profile lacks something the posting wants, say what the seeker would need to supply instead of writing it.
  - interview_prep: a list of strings: three to five stories taken from the profile, each as "Requirement it answers: situation, action, result (the profile's own numbers), what was learned", then one string for each of the two or three questions the interviewer is most likely to probe given the gaps above, each with the honest answer the profile supports, then one string naming the profile project to walk through as a case study, the order to tell it in, and the requirements it answers.

Write in English.
